const fs_ = ['frame-shear-1e-7', 'frame-shear-5e-7', 'frame-shear-9e-7'];
row("sketch({origin,u,v}) with u/v slightly off orthogonal or off unit length, inside the language's 1e-6 tolerance", 'wrong',
  `${cmd('round3')} The interpreter accepts a frame whose dot(u,v) or |u|-1 is up to 1e-6 and stores it as given. Rect 30 x 20 pulled 10 on v=[eps,1,0], hand-derived bbox x in +-(15+10*eps): eps=1e-7 / 5e-7 / 9e-7: OCCT and the closed form take the frame as given (bbox x = +-${R('frame-shear-1e-7').occt.bbox[3]} / ${R('frame-shear-5e-7').occt.bbox[3]} / ${R('frame-shear-9e-7').occt.bbox[3]}); SolidCraft returns x = +-15 exactly (it orthonormalises), so the bbox differs by ${fs_.map((i) => e(R(i).truck.dBboxOcct)).join(' / ')} relative (up to ${(Math.max(...fs_.map((i) => Math.abs(R(i).truck.bbox[3] - R(i).occt.bbox[3])))).toExponential(1)} mm absolute), above the 1e-9 planar bar; volume is equal (6000). |u| = 1+9e-7: OCCT 6000.0054 (as given), SolidCraft 6000, brep-rs 6000, closed form 6000.0054: SolidCraft differs by ${e(R('frame-u-length-9e-7').truck.dVolOcct)}. brep-rs follows OCCT on the shear (bbox x = +-15.000001..15.000009) but, like SolidCraft, ignores the length. Explanation: SolidCraft rebuilds the frame as an orthonormal basis, the language promises to use the frame as given. Immaterial for any real part (<= 1.4e-5 mm), but it breaks the planar bar and the 'exact frame' promise. Fix either side: tighten the language tolerance to 1e-9, or let the translator send an orthonormalised frame and state it.`);

